WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.898 --> 00:00:05.940
STEPHEN OSADETZ: From the printing
press to the mass production of books,

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.143
there's a long history of scholars
adapting to new technologies.

00:00:09.143 --> 00:00:11.310
We're used to dealing with
change in the humanities.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:15.480
In the last 40 or 50 years, the most
significant advance in our field

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.750
has been the digitization of books.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.110
This is a wonderful benefit,
but it's also a challenge.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.380
Given that you could have hundreds or
thousands of books available to you

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.005
in an instant, where do you even start?

00:00:27.005 --> 00:00:28.755
CHRISTINE FERNSEBNER
ESLAO: What can we do

00:00:28.755 --> 00:00:30.840
with books now that we couldn't before?

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.350
You can use tools from data science
to explore the record of human culture

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.810
in ways that just wouldn't
have been possible before.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.450
Whether you're a student wanting
to expand your skill set,

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.760
a librarian supporting
new modes of research,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.330
or a journalist who has just received
a massive cache of leaked emails,

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.550
this course will show
you how to draw insights

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.857
from thousands of documents at once.

00:00:49.857 --> 00:00:52.440
COLE CRAWFORD: In this course,
you'll learned the fundamentals

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.620
of text analysis, a set of
techniques for manipulating

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:59.560
the written word that stand at the
core of the digital humanities.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.340
You will learn how, with a
few simple lines of code,

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.060
to make use of the metadata and
zero in on what matters most.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.030
CEILYN BOYD: Research data is evidence.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:15.630
It's evidence that you will use to make
your case to the scientific community.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.380
STEPHEN OSADETZ: By
the end of the course,

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.797
you'll be able to apply what
you learned to what interests

00:01:19.797 --> 00:01:24.630
you most, whether that's contemporary
speeches, journalism, novels,

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:29.540
or even art objects, material from
across the human cultural record.

